window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert12-bio-biotech-principles","kind":"practice","exam":"NEET","year":null,"title":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes · Practice","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","subject":"Biology","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-001","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":1,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Disarmed Ti plasmid\n(B) Disarmed retrovirus\n(C) β-galactosidase coding sequence\n(D) Chromogenic substrate\nList-II\n(I) Vector used to deliver desirable genes into animal cells\n(II) Gives blue colour in colonies whose plasmid has no insert\n(III) Vector used to deliver genes of interest into a variety of plants\n(IV) Site where the insertion of foreign DNA causes insertional inactivation\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The modified Ti plasmid of Agrobacterium tumefaciens delivers genes into plants, and disarmed retroviruses deliver genes into animal cells. Foreign DNA inserted in the β-galactosidase coding sequence inactivates the gene. The chromogenic substrate turns blue only where the enzyme is intact, i.e. in non-recombinant colonies.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-002","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":2,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"The primers used in a polymerase chain reaction are best described as:","image":"","options":["Small chemically synthesised oligonucleotides complementary to regions of the target DNA","Restriction enzymes that cut the DNA at palindromic sites","Long RNA molecules isolated from the host cell","Plasmids that carry the gene of interest into the host"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Primers are short, chemically synthesised oligonucleotides that are complementary to specific regions of the template DNA. They give DNA polymerase a starting point for extension. They are neither enzymes nor vectors.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-003","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":3,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"In the first recombinant DNA experiment, which enzyme joined the cut antibiotic resistance gene to the plasmid vector?","image":"","options":["Restriction endonuclease","DNA polymerase","Exonuclease","DNA ligase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"DNA ligase acts on cut DNA molecules and joins their ends, producing the recombinant plasmid. Restriction enzymes only cut the DNA, and DNA polymerase copies DNA.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-004","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":4,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A DNA segment contains the EcoRI site shown below:\n5' —— GAATTC —— 3'\n3' —— CTTAAG —— 5'\nEcoRI cuts each strand between G and A. Which option correctly describes the ends of the two fragments formed?","image":"","options":["Each fragment carries a single-stranded 5'-AATT-3' overhang, and the two overhangs are complementary to each other","Both fragments have ends with no overhang, so ligase cannot use base pairing to align them","Each fragment carries a two-nucleotide 5'-AT-3' overhang","Each fragment carries a five-nucleotide 5'-CTTAA-3' overhang"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Cutting G|AATTC on the top strand and CTTAA|G on the bottom strand leaves AATT unpaired, read 5' → 3', on both fragments. AATT is self-complementary, so either fragment can hydrogen bond with any EcoRI-cut end, including a vector cut with EcoRI. A common trap is to count the overhang wrongly or to read it in the 3' → 5' direction.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-005","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":5,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Of the two enzymes isolated from Escherichia coli in 1963 that restricted the growth of bacteriophage, only one was named restriction endonuclease.\nReason (R): One of these enzymes added methyl groups to DNA, while the other cut DNA.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Only the enzyme that cut DNA was called restriction endonuclease. The other enzyme added methyl groups to DNA and did not cut it. So R explains why only one of the two got this name.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-006","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":6,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"In the chromogenic selection method, foreign DNA is inserted within the coding sequence of the $\\beta$-galactosidase gene of a vector. On a medium containing a chromogenic substrate, how do bacterial colonies carrying recombinant plasmids appear?","image":"","options":["Blue","Colourless","Bright orange","Red"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The insert inactivates the $\\beta$-galactosidase gene, so the enzyme is not made and the substrate is not converted to a coloured product. The colonies therefore stay colourless. Blue colonies carry plasmids without an insert.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-007","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":7,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Besides being the point where replication starts, which other role does the origin of replication (ori) of a cloning vector play?","image":"","options":["It allows the host cell to be selected on antibiotic medium","It controls the copy number of the linked DNA","It provides the recognition site for restriction enzymes","It makes the host cell competent to take up DNA"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The ori also controls the copy number of any DNA linked to it. To get many copies of the target DNA, you clone it in a vector whose ori supports a high copy number. Selection comes from markers, and competence comes from treatment with divalent cations.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-008","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":8,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(a) DNA cannot pass through cell membranes because it is hydrophilic.\n(b) Competent bacterial cells are prepared using a specific concentration of a divalent cation such as calcium.\n(c) During heat shock, cells are placed briefly at 42°C.\n(d) The gene gun uses DNA-coated microparticles of silver or platinum.\n(e) Disarmed retroviruses are used to deliver desirable genes into animal cells.\n(f) Micro-injection delivers recombinant DNA into the cytoplasm of plant cells.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statements (a), (b), (c) and (e) are correct. The gene gun uses gold or tungsten particles, not silver or platinum, so (d) is wrong. Micro-injection places DNA in the nucleus of animal cells, not the cytoplasm of plant cells, so (f) is wrong.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-009","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":9,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"When a protein-encoding gene is expressed in a heterologous host, the protein produced is called a:","image":"","options":["Recombinant protein","Native protein","Chimeric enzyme","Reporter protein"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A protein produced from a gene expressed in a host of a different species (a heterologous host) is a recombinant protein. Native proteins are made by the organism's own genes.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-010","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":10,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, biotechnology deals with techniques that use:","image":"","options":["Live organisms or enzymes from organisms to produce products and processes useful to humans","Only synthetic chemical reactions to manufacture drugs","Only physical instruments to observe living cells","Only genetically modified animals for laboratory research"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Biotechnology uses live organisms or enzymes derived from them to make products and processes useful to humans. The other options are either too narrow or describe no biological agent at all.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-011","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":11,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Foreign DNA is ligated at the BamH I site of the vector pBR322, and E. coli cells are transformed. Four colonies were tested for growth on two media:\nColony P: grows on ampicillin, grows on tetracycline\nColony Q: grows on ampicillin, does not grow on tetracycline\nColony R: does not grow on ampicillin, grows on tetracycline\nColony S: does not grow on either\nWhich colony carries the recombinant plasmid?","image":"","options":["Colony P","Colony Q","Colony R","Colony S"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The BamH I site lies inside the tetracycline resistance gene, so inserting foreign DNA inactivates it, while the ampicillin resistance gene stays intact. Recombinants therefore grow on ampicillin but not on tetracycline. Colony P carries a non-recombinant plasmid, and colony S is untransformed.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-012","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":12,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Bacteriophages have very high copy numbers of their genome within bacterial cells because of their high number per cell.\nStatement II: All plasmids are present in at least 15–100 copies per bacterial cell.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Phages do reach very high copy numbers within the host. Plasmid copy numbers vary: some have only one or two copies per cell, while others have 15–100 or more, so Statement II is wrong.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-013","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":13,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The Ti plasmid of Agrobacterium tumefaciens is used as a cloning vector to transfer genes of interest into a variety of plants.\nReason (R): The Ti plasmid has been modified so that it is no longer pathogenic to plants but can still use its mechanism to deliver genes.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The disarmed Ti plasmid can be used as a vector because its tumour-causing ability has been removed while its gene-delivery machinery is kept. R therefore directly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-014","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":14,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about making a bacterial host competent for transformation is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Bacterial cells are treated with a specific concentration of a divalent cation such as calcium","Cells mixed with recombinant DNA are kept on ice, given a brief heat shock at $42^{\\circ}C$, and then returned to ice","DNA is a hydrophobic molecule and therefore cannot pass through cell membranes on its own","Calcium treatment increases the efficiency with which DNA enters the bacterium through pores in its cell wall"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"DNA cannot cross cell membranes because it is hydrophilic, not hydrophobic. All the other statements correctly describe the calcium treatment and heat-shock method.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-015","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":15,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (method of introducing alien DNA) with List-II (description).\nList-I:\n(A) Micro-injection\n(B) Biolistics\n(C) Disarmed pathogen vector\n(D) Divalent cation treatment followed by heat shock\nList-II:\n(I) Bacterial cells are made competent and then briefly placed at 42°C\n(II) Recombinant DNA is directly injected into the nucleus of an animal cell\n(III) Plant cells are bombarded with DNA-coated micro-particles\n(IV) The vector infects the cell and transfers the recombinant DNA into the host\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Micro-injection places DNA directly into the animal cell nucleus, and biolistics bombards plant cells with coated particles. Disarmed pathogens deliver DNA by infecting the cell, while Ca2+ treatment with heat shock transforms bacteria. A common trap is swapping micro-injection (animal nucleus) with biolistics (plants).","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-016","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":16,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"In 1963, two enzymes that restricted the growth of bacteriophage in Escherichia coli were isolated. One of them added methyl groups to DNA. What was the other enzyme, which cut DNA, called?","image":"","options":["DNA ligase","Restriction endonuclease","Exonuclease","DNA polymerase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Of the two phage-restricting enzymes isolated in 1963, the one that cut DNA was named restriction endonuclease. The other enzyme methylated DNA. Ligase joins DNA fragments rather than cutting them.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-017","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":17,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(a) Making curd, bread and wine are microbe-mediated processes that can be regarded as forms of biotechnology in the broad sense.\n(b) In its restricted present-day sense, biotechnology refers to processes that use genetically modified organisms to achieve such products on a larger scale.\n(c) Bioprocess engineering requires a sterile ambience so that only the desired microbe or eukaryotic cell grows in large quantities.\n(d) Asexual reproduction permits variation, whereas sexual reproduction preserves the genetic information.\n(e) The definition of biotechnology given by the European Federation of Biotechnology (EFB) covers only modern molecular biotechnology and excludes the traditional view.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statements (a), (b) and (c) agree with NCERT. Statement (d) reverses the roles: sexual reproduction permits variation, while asexual reproduction preserves genetic information. Statement (e) is wrong because the EFB definition covers both the traditional view and modern molecular biotechnology.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-018","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":18,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A good cloning vector should have very few, preferably a single, recognition site for a commonly used restriction enzyme. The main reason is that:","image":"","options":["A single site prevents the host from degrading the vector","Several sites would cut the vector into many fragments and complicate gene cloning","Restriction enzymes cannot act on DNA that has more than one recognition site","A single site increases the copy number of the vector"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"If the vector has more than one recognition site, the enzyme cuts it into several fragments, which complicates gene cloning. Copy number depends on ori, not on the number of cloning sites.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-019","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":19,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A student cuts the source DNA containing the gene of interest with EcoRI and cuts the plasmid vector with BamHI. She then mixes the two and adds DNA ligase. What is the most likely outcome, according to the principle described in NCERT?","image":"","options":["A recombinant plasmid forms efficiently, because ligase joins any two DNA ends","A recombinant plasmid will normally not form, because the two DNAs were not cut with the same restriction enzyme and so lack matching sticky ends","A recombinant plasmid forms, but only after ribonuclease treatment","The gene of interest is amplified a billion times inside the tube"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Recombinant DNA forms when the source DNA and the vector carry the same kind of sticky ends, which normally requires cutting both with the same restriction enzyme. Ligase seals ends that pair up; it does not create complementarity, and amplification happens only in PCR.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-020","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":20,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A restriction digest gives four DNA fragments of 500 bp, 1500 bp, 3000 bp and 8000 bp. They are separated by agarose gel electrophoresis. Which fragment's band will lie closest to the well where the sample was loaded?","image":"","options":["500 bp","1500 bp","3000 bp","8000 bp"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The agarose gel acts like a sieve, so smaller fragments move farther towards the anode. The largest fragment (8000 bp) moves the least and stays nearest the well.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-021","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":21,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Restriction enzymes are classified as endonucleases.\nReason (R): Restriction enzymes remove nucleotides one by one from the free ends of a DNA molecule.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Restriction enzymes are indeed endonucleases, so A is true. Endonucleases cut at specific positions within the DNA. Removing nucleotides from the ends is what exonucleases do, so R is false.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-022","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":22,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A foreign DNA fragment is ligated at the BamH I site of the vector pBR322, and the transformed E. coli cells are plated. Which of the following correctly describes the growth of cells carrying the recombinant plasmid?","image":"","options":["They grow on both ampicillin and tetracycline media","They grow on ampicillin medium but not on tetracycline medium","They grow on tetracycline medium but not on ampicillin medium","They grow on neither ampicillin nor tetracycline medium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The BamH I site lies within the tetracycline resistance gene, so the insert inactivates tet^R while amp^R stays functional. Recombinants therefore grow on ampicillin but not on tetracycline. Non-recombinants grow on both media.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-023","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":23,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When E. coli cells exposed to a plasmid carrying the ampicillin resistance gene are spread on agar containing ampicillin, untransformed cells die.\nReason (R): Normal E. coli cells do not carry resistance against ampicillin.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Normal E. coli lacks ampicillin resistance, so only cells that took up the ampR-bearing plasmid survive on ampicillin. This is why the ampicillin resistance gene works as a selectable marker. R therefore directly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-024","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":24,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Bioprocess engineering, one of the two core techniques behind modern biotechnology, mainly involves:","image":"","options":["Altering the chemistry of DNA and RNA to change the phenotype of a host","Maintaining a sterile, contamination-free ambience so that only the desired microbe or eukaryotic cell grows in large quantities","Crossing two plant varieties to combine their desirable traits","Separating DNA fragments according to their size on an agarose gel"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Bioprocess engineering keeps chemical engineering processes free of microbial contamination. This lets only the desired cells grow on a large scale to make products such as antibiotics, vaccines and enzymes. Altering genetic material is genetic engineering, not bioprocess engineering.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-025","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":25,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (material to be removed or broken during DNA isolation) with List-II (enzyme used).\nList-I:\nA. Bacterial cell wall\nB. Fungal cell wall\nC. Histone proteins\nD. Plant cell wall\nList-II:\nI. Protease\nII. Cellulase\nIII. Lysozyme\nIV. Chitinase","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Lysozyme breaks bacterial cell walls, chitinase breaks fungal walls, protease removes histones and other proteins, and cellulase breaks plant cell walls. A common trap is swapping the enzymes for bacteria and fungi.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-026","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":26,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In the chromogenic substrate method, blue colonies are identified as recombinant colonies.\nStatement II: Insertion of foreign DNA within the coding sequence of $\\beta$-galactosidase inactivates the gene for this enzyme.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement II is correct. Because the insert inactivates $\\beta$-galactosidase, recombinant colonies produce no colour. Blue colonies are the non-recombinants, so Statement I is wrong.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-027","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":27,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements regarding large-scale production of a recombinant product is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The separation and purification steps carried out after the biosynthetic stage are collectively called downstream processing.","In a continuous culture, spent medium is drained from one side while fresh medium is added from the other, so that cells stay in the stationary phase, where biomass is maximum.","The downstream processing and quality control testing required differ from one product to another.","A stirred-tank bioreactor is usually cylindrical or has a curved base, which helps in mixing the reactor contents."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Continuous culture keeps cells in their physiologically most active log (exponential) phase, not the stationary phase, and this is why it yields a larger biomass. The other three statements match NCERT.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-028","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":28,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"When a bacterial cell is lysed, RNA is released along with the DNA. Which treatment removes this RNA contamination?","image":"","options":["Treatment with protease","Treatment with lysozyme","Treatment with DNA ligase","Treatment with ribonuclease"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Ribonuclease breaks down RNA, leaving the DNA intact. Protease removes proteins, lysozyme digests the bacterial cell wall, and ligase joins DNA ends.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-029","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":29,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In agarose gel electrophoresis, smaller DNA fragments move farther from the well than larger fragments.\nReason (R): Agarose is a synthetic polymer that separates DNA fragments on the basis of their charge alone.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Smaller fragments do travel farther because agarose gel acts as a sieve and separates fragments by size. The reason is false on two counts: agarose is a natural polymer extracted from seaweeds, and separation depends on size, not on charge alone.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-030","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":30,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"After electrophoresis, a gel stained with ethidium bromide is exposed to UV light. How do the DNA fragments appear?","image":"","options":["As bright orange bands","As blue coloured bands","As dark black bands visible in daylight","As green fluorescent spots only at the wells"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"DNA stained with ethidium bromide fluoresces as bright orange bands under UV radiation. Blue colour is associated with the β-galactosidase chromogenic test, not with gel staining.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-031","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":31,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are methods for introducing recombinant DNA into host cells?\nMicro-injection, Biolistics, Elution, Calcium chloride treatment followed by heat shock, Spooling, Use of disarmed pathogen vectors, Gel electrophoresis","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Four of these are DNA transfer methods: micro-injection, biolistics (gene gun), Ca²⁺ treatment with heat shock, and disarmed pathogen vectors. Elution, spooling and gel electrophoresis are steps for separating or recovering DNA, not for transferring it.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-032","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":32,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(a) Agrobacterium tumefaciens is a pathogen of several dicot plants.\n(b) T-DNA delivered by Agrobacterium transforms normal plant cells into tumour cells.\n(c) The tumour cells are directed to produce chemicals required by the pathogen.\n(d) The natural Ti plasmid is used as a plant vector without any modification.\n(e) Retroviruses in animals can transform normal cells into cancerous cells.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statements (a), (b), (c) and (e) agree with NCERT. Statement (d) is wrong because the Ti plasmid must be modified (disarmed) so that it is no longer pathogenic before it can be used as a vector.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-033","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":33,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Hind II was the first restriction endonuclease to be isolated and characterised whose functioning depended on a specific DNA nucleotide sequence.\nStatement II: Hind II was isolated in 1963, together with the methylating enzyme from Escherichia coli.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Hind II was indeed the first sequence-dependent restriction endonuclease to be characterised. However, it was isolated five years after the 1963 discovery, so Statement II is wrong.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-034","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":34,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A student cuts a plasmid vector with EcoRI, which leaves AATT overhangs. He cuts the source DNA containing his gene with BamHI, which recognises 5'—GGATCC—3', cuts between G and G, and leaves GATC overhangs. He mixes the two and adds DNA ligase. The most likely outcome is that:","image":"","options":["the gene is efficiently inserted, because both enzymes recognise six-base palindromes","the gene is efficiently inserted, because ligase joins any two DNA ends with equal ease","the recombinant vector is not formed efficiently, because the AATT and GATC overhangs are not complementary and cannot base pair","the BamHI fragment is converted into an EcoRI fragment by ligase before joining"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Sticky ends join only when their overhangs are complementary, which is why the vector and source DNA are cut with the same enzyme. Both sites being palindromes does not make their overhangs match.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-035","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":35,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about introducing recombinant DNA into host cells are correct?\n(i) DNA is hydrophilic and therefore cannot pass through cell membranes on its own.\n(ii) Bacterial cells are made competent by treating them with a specific concentration of a divalent cation such as calcium.\n(iii) During transformation, cells with recombinant DNA are kept on ice, given a brief heat shock at 42°C, and then returned to ice.\n(iv) In micro-injection, recombinant DNA is injected directly into the nucleus of a plant cell.\n(v) In biolistics, cells are bombarded with high-velocity micro-particles of gold or tungsten coated with DNA.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statements (i), (ii), (iii) and (v) are correct. Statement (iv) is wrong because micro-injection delivers DNA into the nucleus of an animal cell. Biolistics (gene gun) is the method suited to plants.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-036","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":36,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A vector carries an ampicillin resistance gene and a $\\beta$-galactosidase gene. Foreign DNA is ligated at a restriction site inside the $\\beta$-galactosidase gene, and E. coli cells are transformed. The cells are spread on a single plate containing both ampicillin and a chromogenic substrate. Which colonies should be picked as recombinants?","image":"","options":["Blue colonies growing on the plate","Colourless colonies growing on the plate","Cells that fail to grow on the plate","All colonies on the plate, since every transformant carries the insert"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Ampicillin kills untransformed E. coli, so only transformants grow on this plate. Among them, colonies whose $\\beta$-galactosidase gene is inactivated by the insert stay colourless and are the recombinants. Blue colonies carry the vector without an insert.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-037","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":37,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are carried out on a biotechnological product after completion of the biosynthetic stage and before it is marketed?\nSeparation; Purification; Formulation with suitable preservatives; Clinical trials (for drugs); Quality control testing; Heat shock of competent cells; Primer annealing","image":"","options":["3","4","5","7"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Separation, purification, formulation, clinical trials and quality control all follow the biosynthetic stage, which makes five steps. Heat shock belongs to transformation and primer annealing belongs to PCR; both come much earlier in the workflow.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-038","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":38,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"When a protein-encoding gene from one organism is expressed in a different (heterologous) host organism, the protein produced is called a:","image":"","options":["Recombinant protein","Restriction protein","Reporter protein","Chimeric enzyme"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT calls a protein a recombinant protein when its gene is expressed in a heterologous host. A reporter enzyme is used only to monitor transformation, not to name the product.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-039","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":39,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"An agarose gel has four lanes. Lane 1 contains undigested linear DNA, and lanes 2 to 4 contain the same DNA digested with restriction enzymes. The gel is stained and viewed. Consider the following statements:\n(i) The samples were loaded in wells at the cathode (negative) end of the gel.\n(ii) The undigested linear DNA in lane 1 travels farther than any of its own restriction fragments.\n(iii) The bands can be seen in visible light even without staining.\n(iv) Cutting a band out of the gel and extracting DNA from the gel piece is called elution.\n(v) In a digested lane, the band nearest the well represents the largest fragment.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statements (i), (iv) and (v) are correct, so the answer is 3. DNA moves from wells at the cathode end towards the anode, larger pieces stay nearer the well, and extracting a band from the gel is elution. Statement (ii) is wrong because intact DNA is larger than its fragments and moves less. Statement (iii) is wrong because DNA is seen only after ethidium bromide staining and UV exposure.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-040","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":40,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following double-stranded sequences (only one strand shown, 5' → 3') are palindromic?\nGAATTC, GGATCC, AAGCTT, GACTTC, GATATC, AGCTTA","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A sequence is palindromic if it equals its own reverse complement. GAATTC, GGATCC, AAGCTT and GATATC pass this test. GACTTC (reverse complement GAAGTC) and AGCTTA (reverse complement TAAGCT) do not.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-041","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":41,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A mosquito acts as an insect vector that carries the malarial parasite into the human body. In the same way, a plasmid serves as a vector to carry foreign DNA into a host. Which description of a plasmid matches NCERT?","image":"","options":["Autonomously replicating circular extra-chromosomal DNA","Linear DNA segment that replicates only after integrating into the chromosome","Circular RNA molecule found in bacterial cytoplasm","Protein coat that encloses a viral genome"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT defines a plasmid as autonomously replicating circular extra-chromosomal DNA. Because it replicates on its own, it can ferry and multiply the DNA attached to it.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-042","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":42,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Each sequence below is one strand of a double-stranded DNA, written $5' \\rightarrow 3'$. How many of them are palindromic in the sense used for restriction sites?\n5'-GAATTC-3'\n5'-GGATCC-3'\n5'-GATATC-3'\n5'-GTACTC-3'\n5'-AACGTT-3'\n5'-CAGCTC-3'","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A DNA palindrome reads the same on both strands in the $5' \\rightarrow 3'$ direction, so the sequence must equal its reverse complement. This holds for GAATTC, GGATCC, GATATC and AACGTT, giving 4. GTACTC (reverse complement GAGTAC) and CAGCTC (reverse complement GAGCTG) fail the test.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-043","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":43,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Foreign DNA is ligated at the Sal I site, which lies within tetR, of pBR322. Transformed E. coli cells are spread on an ampicillin plate, and the colonies are then transferred to a tetracycline plate. Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: Colonies that grow on the ampicillin plate but are absent from the tetracycline plate carry the recombinant plasmid.\nStatement II: Selection based on inactivation of an antibiotic resistance gene is considered cumbersome because it requires plating on two plates with different antibiotics.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"An insert at Sal I disrupts tetR, so recombinants resist ampicillin but are killed by tetracycline. Statement I is therefore correct. Statement II is also correct: needing two antibiotic plates is why colour-based markers using $\\beta$-galactosidase were developed.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-044","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":44,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"EcoRI cuts its site 5'—GAATTC—3' between G and A on each strand. The ends of the resulting fragments will be:","image":"","options":["blunt ends with no single-stranded region","two-base single-stranded overhangs of AT","four-base single-stranded overhangs reading AATT (5' → 3') on each fragment","six-base single-stranded overhangs reading GAATTC"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Cutting between G and A on both strands leaves the central AATT unpaired on each fragment as a 5' overhang. Because the cut lies away from the centre of the palindrome, the ends are sticky rather than blunt.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-045","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":45,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"In each PCR cycle shown in Figure 9.6, there is a step in which short, chemically synthesised oligonucleotides bind to complementary regions on the single-stranded template DNA. What is this step called?","image":"","options":["Denaturation","Primer annealing","Extension","Ligation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The binding of primers to complementary sequences on the separated template strands is called primer annealing. Denaturation separates the strands, and extension is the step in which DNA polymerase lengthens the primers. Ligation is not a step of PCR.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-046","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":46,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"During DNA isolation, purified DNA comes out of the suspension as a collection of fine threads that can be removed by spooling. What causes the DNA to separate out in this way?","image":"","options":["Addition of protease","Addition of chilled ethanol","Exposure to UV light after staining with ethidium bromide","Heat shock at 42°C"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Adding chilled ethanol precipitates the purified DNA as fine threads, which are wound out by spooling (Fig 9.5). Heat shock is used for transformation, and ethidium bromide with UV is used to see DNA in a gel.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-047","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":47,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following can be regarded as part of biotechnology (in its broad sense)?\nMaking curd from milk\nProducing wine by fermentation\nIn vitro fertilisation leading to a test-tube baby\nDeveloping a DNA vaccine\nCorrecting a defective gene\nSmelting iron ore in a blast furnace","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Five items count as biotechnology: curd, wine, IVF, DNA vaccines and correcting a defective gene all use organisms, cells or their parts. Smelting iron ore is a purely metallurgical process with no biological agent, so it is excluded.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-048","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":48,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The ampicillin resistance gene on a vector is called a selectable marker because, in the presence of ampicillin, it allows transformed E. coli to grow while untransformed cells die.\nStatement II: Plating transformed cells on ampicillin alone is enough to separate cells carrying a recombinant plasmid from cells carrying a non-recombinant plasmid, when the insert lies outside the ampR gene.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: ampicillin selects transformants from non-transformants. Statement II is incorrect because recombinant and non-recombinant plasmids both carry an intact ampR gene. Both kinds of cells therefore grow on ampicillin, and a second screen (insertional inactivation, e.g., on tetracycline or β-galactosidase) is needed to identify recombinants.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-049","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":49,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Bioprocess engineering requires a sterile, contamination-free ambience so that only the desired microbe or eukaryotic cell grows in large quantities.\nStatement II: Traditional hybridisation in plant and animal breeding allows only the desired genes to be introduced, without any undesirable genes.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I correctly defines bioprocess engineering. Statement II is wrong because traditional hybridisation often brings in undesirable genes along with the desired ones. It is genetic engineering that overcomes this limitation.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-050","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":50,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"The thermostable DNA polymerase used in the polymerase chain reaction (PCR) is isolated from which organism?","image":"","options":["Escherichia coli","Thermus aquaticus","Agrobacterium tumefaciens","Salmonella typhimurium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"PCR uses a heat-stable DNA polymerase (Taq polymerase) obtained from the bacterium Thermus aquaticus. E. coli and Salmonella are linked to early cloning work, and Agrobacterium is the source of the Ti plasmid vector.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-051","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":51,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following has been disarmed and is now used as a vector to deliver desirable genes into animal cells?","image":"","options":["Ti plasmid","Retroviruses","pBR322","Bacteriophage infecting E. coli"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Retroviruses can naturally transform normal animal cells into cancerous cells. Disarmed retroviruses are used to carry genes into animal cells. The Ti plasmid is the corresponding vector for plants.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-052","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":52,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A piece of alien DNA transferred into an organism usually fails to multiply in the progeny cells unless it becomes part of a chromosome or is linked to a suitable sequence.\nReason (R): Replication of DNA is initiated only from a specific sequence called the origin of replication.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Alien DNA lacking an origin of replication cannot replicate on its own. Integration into a chromosome, or linkage to an ori, lets it multiply and be inherited, so R directly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-053","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":53,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"In a cloning vector, which sequence decides how many copies of the linked foreign DNA will be produced inside the host cell?","image":"","options":["Selectable marker gene","Origin of replication (ori)","Recognition site for a restriction enzyme","Sticky end of the inserted DNA"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Replication starts at ori, and ori also controls the copy number of any DNA linked to it. A vector whose ori supports a high copy number is chosen when many copies of the target DNA are needed. Selectable markers only help identify transformants.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-054","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":54,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A researcher wants to isolate DNA from leaf tissue. Which enzyme should be used first to break open the plant cells and release their contents?","image":"","options":["Lysozyme","Chitinase","Cellulase","Ribonuclease"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Plant cells have a cellulose cell wall, so cellulase is used to break them open. Lysozyme is used for bacteria and chitinase for fungi, while ribonuclease only removes RNA after lysis.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-055","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":55,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Which organisation gave the definition of biotechnology as 'the integration of natural science and organisms, cells, parts thereof, and molecular analogues for products and services'?","image":"","options":["World Health Organization (WHO)","European Federation of Biotechnology (EFB)","Genetic Engineering Approval Committee (GEAC)","Indian Council of Medical Research (ICMR)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"This definition comes from the European Federation of Biotechnology. It covers both the traditional view and modern molecular biotechnology. GEAC is an Indian regulatory body and did not frame this definition.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-056","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":56,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A restriction endonuclease recognises a palindromic sequence, one that reads the same on both strands when each is read in the $5' \\rightarrow 3'$ direction. Which of the following double-stranded sequences (top strand written $5' \\rightarrow 3'$) is a palindrome of this kind?","image":"","options":["$5'$-GAATTG-$3'$","$5'$-GGAATT-$3'$","$5'$-GGATCC-$3'$","$5'$-GCATGG-$3'$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The complementary strand of GGATCC, read $5' \\rightarrow 3'$, is also GGATCC, so it is a true DNA palindrome. The other sequences give CAATTC, AATTCC and CCATGC respectively. The common trap is checking only whether the top strand reads the same backwards, rather than comparing it with its complementary strand.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-057","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":57,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Which option gives the three basic steps of genetically modifying an organism in the correct order?","image":"","options":["Introduction of DNA into host → identification of desirable genes → maintenance and transfer to progeny","Identification of DNA with desirable genes → introduction into host → maintenance in host and transfer to progeny","Identification of desirable genes → maintenance in host → introduction into host","Maintenance of DNA in host → identification of desirable genes → introduction into host"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"First the DNA with desirable genes is identified, then it is introduced into the host. Finally it is maintained in the host and passed on to the progeny. DNA cannot be maintained before it has been introduced.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-058","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":58,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following:\nRestriction enzymes, DNA ligase, polymerase enzymes, vectors, host organism, chitinase\nHow many of these are listed by NCERT as the key tools needed to accomplish recombinant DNA technology?","image":"","options":["Three","Four","Five","Six"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT names five key tools: restriction enzymes, polymerase enzymes, ligases, vectors and the host organism. Chitinase is only used to break open fungal cells during DNA isolation, so it is not one of the key tools.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-059","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":59,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (component of a stirred-tank bioreactor) with List-II (its role).\nList-I:\n(A) Agitator (stirrer)\n(B) Sampling port\n(C) Sparger\n(D) Foam control system\nList-II:\n(I) Withdrawal of small volumes of culture periodically\n(II) Even mixing and oxygen availability throughout the vessel\n(III) Controlling froth formed during culture\n(IV) Bubbling sterile air into the culture\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The stirrer gives uniform mixing and oxygen distribution, and sampling ports let small culture volumes be withdrawn. The sparger bubbles sterile air into the culture, and the foam control system manages foaming. Option B swaps the roles of the agitator and the sparger.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-060","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":60,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): After transformation with pBR322, E. coli cells that have taken up the plasmid can be selected by plating on an ampicillin-containing medium.\nReason (R): Normal E. coli cells do not carry resistance against ampicillin.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Untransformed E. coli is sensitive to ampicillin, so only cells carrying the ampR gene of the plasmid survive on ampicillin. This is exactly why ampicillin resistance works as a selectable marker, so R correctly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-061","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":61,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A piece of alien DNA that gets integrated into the genome of the recipient may multiply and be inherited along with the host DNA.\nReason (R): After integration, the alien DNA becomes part of a chromosome, which carries an origin of replication that initiates replication.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Integrated DNA is copied because it is now part of a chromosome. The chromosome's origin of replication starts replication, so R directly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-062","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":62,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"In the E. coli cloning vector pBR322, the gene labelled 'rop' codes for:","image":"","options":["Proteins involved in the replication of the plasmid","The enzyme that gives resistance to tetracycline","A restriction endonuclease that cuts the plasmid","The enzyme $\\beta$-galactosidase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In the pBR322 map, rop codes for proteins involved in the replication of the plasmid. Tetracycline resistance is coded by tetR. The plasmid carries no gene for a restriction enzyme or for $\\beta$-galactosidase.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-063","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":63,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Ethidium bromide\n(B) Chilled ethanol\n(C) Elution\n(D) Thermus aquaticus\nList-II\n(I) Source of thermostable DNA polymerase\n(II) Extracting separated DNA bands from the gel piece\n(III) Staining DNA so it can be seen under UV light\n(IV) Precipitating purified DNA\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Ethidium bromide stains DNA so it appears as orange bands under UV light. Chilled ethanol makes purified DNA precipitate out. Elution is extracting DNA from the cut gel piece. Thermus aquaticus supplies the heat-stable polymerase used in PCR.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-064","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":64,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Making multiple copies of the antibiotic resistance gene in E. coli was called cloning of the antibiotic resistance gene in E. coli.\nStatement II: In E. coli, the recombinant plasmid replicated using a DNA polymerase carried over from Salmonella along with the plasmid.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Cloning means making multiple identical copies of a template DNA, so Statement I is correct. The recombinant DNA was replicated by the new host's (E. coli's) own DNA polymerase, not an enzyme brought from Salmonella, so Statement II is incorrect.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-065","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":65,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A cloning vector should preferably have only a single recognition site for a commonly used restriction enzyme.\nReason (R): More than one recognition site for the same enzyme in the vector will generate several fragments, which complicates gene cloning.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"If an enzyme cuts the vector at several places, the vector breaks into multiple fragments and cannot simply be reopened to accept the insert. This is exactly why single cloning sites are preferred.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-066","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":66,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following double-stranded DNA sequences (top strand shown 5' → 3') is a palindrome that a restriction endonuclease could recognise?","image":"","options":["5'-GAATTG-3'","5'-AAGCTA-3'","5'-GGATCC-3'","5'-ACGCGA-3'"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The complementary strand of GGATCC, read 5' → 3', is also GGATCC, so it reads the same on both strands. For each of the other options, the complementary strand read 5' → 3' gives a different sequence. The trap is to look for mirror-image letters on a single strand instead of comparing the two strands.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-067","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":67,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"In the restriction enzyme EcoRI, isolated from Escherichia coli RY 13, what does the letter 'R' represent?","image":"","options":["The genus of the bacterium","The species of the bacterium","The strain of the bacterium","The order in which the enzyme was isolated"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In EcoRI, 'E' comes from the genus Escherichia and 'co' from the species coli. 'R' comes from the strain RY 13. The Roman numeral 'I' gives the order of isolation, not the letter R.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-068","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":68,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"The restriction enzyme BamH I was the first such enzyme isolated from strain H of the bacterium Bacillus amyloliquefaciens. Applying the standard naming convention, which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The letter 'B' is derived from the genus Bacillus","The letters 'am' are derived from the species amyloliquefaciens","The letter 'H' indicates the strain of the bacterium","The Roman numeral 'I' indicates that the enzyme has only one recognition site in a vector"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"By convention, the first letter comes from the genus, the next two from the species, and the following letter from the strain. The Roman numeral only indicates the order in which enzymes were isolated from that strain. It says nothing about how many recognition sites the enzyme has in a vector.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-069","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":69,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"In the name of the restriction enzyme EcoRI, the letter 'R' indicates:","image":"","options":["The genus of the bacterium from which it was isolated","The strain of the bacterium from which it was isolated","That it is a restriction enzyme rather than a methylase","The order in which the enzyme was isolated from the bacterium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"EcoRI comes from Escherichia coli strain RY 13. 'E' is from the genus, 'co' is from the species, 'R' is from the strain, and the Roman numeral 'I' gives the order of isolation. A common trap is to read 'R' as 'restriction'.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-070","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":70,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In agarose gel electrophoresis, smaller DNA fragments travel farther from the well than larger fragments.\nReason (R): The agarose gel provides a sieving effect that resolves DNA fragments according to their size.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The agarose matrix acts like a sieve. Small fragments pass through its pores more easily and therefore move farther in a given time. The sieving effect is exactly why separation depends on size.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-071","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":71,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(a) Denaturation\n(b) Primer annealing\n(c) Extension\n(d) Primer\nList-II\n(i) DNA polymerase adds nucleotides using the template\n(ii) Short synthetic oligonucleotide\n(iii) High temperature separates the two DNA strands\n(iv) Oligonucleotides bind to complementary regions of single-stranded DNA\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Denaturation is heat separation of the strands, and annealing is binding of primers to complementary sequences. Extension is synthesis of new DNA by the polymerase, and a primer is a short synthetic oligonucleotide. Swapping annealing with denaturation is the common error.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-072","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":72,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (enzyme used during isolation of DNA) with List-II (its target/use):\nList-I\nA. Lysozyme\nB. Cellulase\nC. Chitinase\nD. Ribonuclease\nList-II\nI. Removal of RNA\nII. Breaking open fungal cells\nIII. Breaking open bacterial cells\nIV. Breaking open plant cells","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Lysozyme digests bacterial cell walls, cellulase acts on plant cell walls and chitinase acts on fungal walls. Ribonuclease removes contaminating RNA. Cellulase and chitinase are easy to swap, so match each to the wall polymer it digests.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-073","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":73,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"In a method used to introduce recombinant DNA into plant cells, the cells are bombarded with high-velocity micro-particles coated with DNA. These micro-particles are made of:","image":"","options":["Gold or tungsten","Silver or copper","Calcium or magnesium","Iron or zinc"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In biolistics (the gene gun method), plant cells are bombarded with DNA-coated micro-particles of gold or tungsten. Calcium is used for a different purpose: it is the divalent cation that makes bacterial cells competent, not a material for micro-particles.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-074","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":74,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements about obtaining and processing a foreign gene product is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Cloning a gene alone is not enough; the recombinant DNA must also be expressed under appropriate conditions to obtain the protein.","The stirrer in a stirred-tank bioreactor helps distribute oxygen evenly throughout the culture.","Downstream processing and quality control testing follow the same standard protocol for every biotechnological product.","A product meant to be used as a drug must undergo thorough clinical trials after formulation."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that downstream processing and quality control testing vary from product to product, so they are not uniform. The other three statements correctly describe gene expression, the stirrer's role in oxygen distribution, and clinical trials for drugs.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-075","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":75,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Restriction endonuclease\n(B) DNA ligase\n(C) Sticky end\n(D) Palindromic sequence\nList-II\n(I) Joins the ends of cut DNA molecules\n(II) Single-stranded overhang that hydrogen bonds with a complementary end\n(III) Base sequence that reads the same on both strands in the same orientation\n(IV) Recognises a specific sequence and cuts both strands in the sugar-phosphate backbone\nChoose the correct answer:","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III","A-I, B-IV, C-II, D-III","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The restriction enzyme recognises and cuts DNA, ligase joins the cut ends, sticky ends are pairing overhangs, and a palindrome reads the same on both strands. The common trap is swapping the roles of the cutting and joining enzymes.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-076","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":76,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A restriction digest produces four DNA fragments of 500 bp, 1500 bp, 3000 bp and 8000 bp. They are run together in one lane of an agarose gel. After staining with ethidium bromide and viewing under UV light, which fragment's band will lie closest to the wells where the sample was loaded?","image":"","options":["500 bp","1500 bp","3000 bp","8000 bp"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Agarose acts as a sieve, so smaller fragments travel farther towards the anode. The largest fragment (8000 bp) moves the least and stays nearest the wells.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-077","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":77,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"In the biolistic (gene gun) method of gene transfer, cells are bombarded with high-velocity microparticles coated with DNA. These microparticles are made of:","image":"","options":["Silver or platinum","Gold or tungsten","Calcium or magnesium","Iron or copper"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Biolistics uses DNA-coated microparticles of gold or tungsten fired at high velocity. NCERT describes this method as especially suitable for plant cells.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-078","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":78,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Agarose is the most commonly used matrix for separating DNA fragments by gel electrophoresis. What is agarose and where is it obtained from?","image":"","options":["A synthetic polymer made from acrylamide","A natural polymer extracted from seaweeds","A protein extracted from animal cartilage","A polysaccharide extracted from fungal cell walls"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"According to NCERT, agarose is a natural polymer extracted from seaweeds. The gel it forms acts as a sieve that separates DNA fragments by size. Acrylamide-based gels are synthetic, and agarose is not obtained from animals or fungi.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-079","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":79,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Bacterial cells must be specially treated before they take up plasmid DNA from their surroundings. According to NCERT, the basic reason DNA cannot pass through cell membranes on its own is that DNA is:","image":"","options":["A hydrophobic molecule","A hydrophilic molecule","A positively charged molecule","Too small to be recognised by membrane pores"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"DNA is hydrophilic, so it cannot cross the lipid cell membrane. Bacteria must therefore be made competent before they take up plasmids. DNA is negatively charged, not positively charged, and it is a large molecule.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-080","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":80,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about isolating and preparing DNA for recombinant DNA work are correct?\n(i) DNA must be free from other macromolecules before it can be cut with restriction enzymes.\n(ii) Breaking the cell releases DNA together with RNA, proteins, polysaccharides and lipids.\n(iii) Chitinase is used to break open bacterial cells.\n(iv) Purified DNA precipitates when chilled ethanol is added.\n(v) The vector DNA is also cut with a restriction enzyme before ligation.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statements (i), (ii), (iv) and (v) are correct. Statement (iii) is wrong because chitinase acts on fungal walls, whereas lysozyme is used for bacteria.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-081","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":81,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A student makes E. coli competent and transforms it with a plasmid carrying the ampicillin resistance gene. By mistake, all the cells are spread on nutrient agar without any ampicillin. What is the most likely outcome?","image":"","options":["No colonies grow, because heat-shocked cells cannot divide without ampicillin","Only transformed cells form colonies, since competence kills untransformed cells","Only untransformed cells grow, because the plasmid is lost without antibiotic","Both transformed and untransformed cells form colonies, so transformants cannot be identified"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Without ampicillin there is no selection pressure, so cells with and without the plasmid both grow. As a result, transformants cannot be told apart from untransformed cells. The selectable marker only works when the antibiotic is present in the medium.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-082","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":82,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements is incorrect?","image":"","options":["The origin of replication in a vector also controls the copy number of the DNA linked to it","In the first artificial recombinant DNA, an antibiotic resistance gene was linked to a plasmid of E. coli, and the recombinant DNA was then transferred into Salmonella typhimurium","In a continuous culture system, used medium is drained out while fresh medium is added, keeping cells in the log/exponential phase","In pBR322, the rop gene codes for proteins involved in replication of the plasmid"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Cohen and Boyer linked the antibiotic resistance gene to a native plasmid of Salmonella typhimurium. The recombinant DNA was then transferred into E. coli, where it replicated. The option reverses the two organisms; all the other statements agree with NCERT.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-083","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":83,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"In a cloning experiment, the foreign DNA is inserted within the coding sequence of the β-galactosidase gene of the vector. The transformed bacteria are plated on a medium containing a chromogenic substrate. The colonies that show no colour are","image":"","options":["colonies whose plasmid carries no insert","colonies with an intact, active β-galactosidase gene","colonies carrying recombinant plasmids, with the β-galactosidase gene inactivated by the insert","colonies whose plasmid has lost its origin of replication"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"An insert within the β-galactosidase coding sequence inactivates the gene (insertional inactivation), so these colonies cannot convert the chromogenic substrate and stay colourless. Plasmids without an insert keep a functional enzyme and give blue colonies.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-084","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":84,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"During agarose gel electrophoresis, DNA fragments move through the gel towards which electrode, and why?","image":"","options":["Towards the cathode, because DNA is positively charged","Towards the anode, because DNA is negatively charged","Towards the anode, because DNA is positively charged","Towards the cathode, because DNA is negatively charged"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The phosphate groups give DNA a negative charge, so in an electric field it migrates to the positive electrode, the anode. Options pairing the charge with the wrong electrode ignore that opposite charges attract.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-085","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":85,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\nA. Genetic engineering\nB. Bioprocess engineering\nC. Sexual reproduction\nD. Asexual reproduction\nList-II\n(i) Preserves the genetic information\n(ii) Alters the chemistry of genetic material and changes the host phenotype\n(iii) Permits variation and unique combinations of genetic set-up\n(iv) Sterile ambience for large-scale growth of only the desired cells\nChoose the correct answer:","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Genetic engineering alters DNA or RNA to change the host phenotype, and bioprocess engineering keeps sterile conditions for large-scale culture. Sexual reproduction generates variation, whereas asexual reproduction conserves genetic information. The common trap is swapping the roles of sexual and asexual reproduction.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-086","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":86,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A breeder crosses a high-yielding wheat variety with a wild relative to obtain a disease-resistance trait. The progeny become disease resistant but also inherit genes for poor grain quality from the wild parent. Which approach would best avoid this problem, and why?","image":"","options":["Propagate the hybrid asexually, because asexual reproduction preserves genetic information","Grow the hybrid in a sterile bioreactor, because bioprocess engineering allows only the desired cells to grow","Use genetic engineering to isolate and transfer only the resistance gene, because it avoids introducing undesirable genes","Perform more rounds of sexual crossing, because sexual reproduction creates new gene combinations"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Genetic engineering, through recombinant DNA, gene cloning and gene transfer, lets one desirable gene be introduced without its unwanted companions. Asexual propagation would preserve both the good and bad genes. Bioprocess engineering deals with large-scale culture, not gene selection. Further crossing only reshuffles genes at random.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-087","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":87,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In the restriction enzyme EcoRI, the letters 'Eco' come from the genus and species of the bacterium, while 'R' comes from the strain.\nStatement II: The Roman numeral at the end of a restriction enzyme's name shows the order in which the enzyme was isolated from that strain of bacteria.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"EcoRI is named from Escherichia coli RY 13. 'E' comes from the genus, 'co' from the species and 'R' from the strain. The Roman numeral shows the order of isolation from that strain, not the number of recognition sites.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-088","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":88,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following components are required in a PCR reaction to amplify a gene of interest?\nTemplate (genomic) DNA, two primers, deoxyribonucleotides, thermostable DNA polymerase, DNA ligase, restriction endonuclease","image":"","options":["Three","Five","Six","Four"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"PCR needs four of these: the template DNA, the two primers, the nucleotides, and the thermostable polymerase that extends the primers. Ligase and restriction enzymes are used later to clone the amplified fragment into a vector, not for the amplification itself.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-089","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":89,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about restriction enzyme action at palindromic sites is incorrect?","image":"","options":["The enzyme cuts both strands between the same two bases on the opposite strands","The enzyme cuts exactly at the centre of the palindrome, so no single-stranded region remains","The single-stranded overhangs produced facilitate the action of DNA ligase","Each restriction endonuclease recognises a specific palindromic nucleotide sequence"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that restriction enzymes cut a little away from the centre of the palindrome. This off-centre cut is what leaves single-stranded sticky ends.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-090","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":90,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A thermostable DNA polymerase makes it possible to carry out many repeated cycles of amplification in PCR.\nReason (R): The thermostable DNA polymerase remains active during the high-temperature denaturation of double-stranded DNA.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Every PCR cycle begins with heat denaturation. Only a polymerase that survives this high temperature can keep working over many cycles, so R directly explains A. An ordinary polymerase would be inactivated in the first cycle.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-091","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":91,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A figure shows an agarose gel after electrophoresis. Lane 1 contains undigested DNA and lanes 2 to 4 contain restriction-digested DNA. All bands lie between the wells at one end and the opposite end of the gel. The sample must have been loaded at the end connected to the:","image":"","options":["Anode, since DNA moves away from the positive electrode","Cathode, since DNA moves away from it towards the anode","Middle of the gel, so that fragments can move in both directions","Anode, since smaller fragments stay close to the well"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Negatively charged DNA migrates towards the anode, so the wells must lie at the cathode (negative) end. The bands then spread from the cathode end towards the anode end according to size.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-092","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":92,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A small piece of foreign DNA is transferred into a host organism. It is not integrated into the host genome and is not linked to any replicating sequence. What is its most likely fate?","image":"","options":["It replicates on its own and is passed to all progeny cells","It will most likely not be able to multiply in the progeny cells of the host","It is converted into a plasmid by the host's DNA ligase","It makes the host cells resistant to antibiotics"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Alien DNA multiplies only when it becomes part of a chromosome or is linked to an origin of replication. A free fragment without these is not copied, so it is not maintained in the progeny.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-093","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":93,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"The step in which separated DNA bands are cut out of the agarose gel and the DNA is extracted from the gel pieces is called:","image":"","options":["Spooling","Elution","Ligation","Annealing"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Cutting out bands and extracting the DNA from the gel is called elution. Spooling removes precipitated DNA after ethanol addition, ligation joins DNA ends, and annealing is a step in PCR.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-094","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":94,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Making curd, bread or wine can be considered biotechnology in a broad sense. In the restricted sense used today, however, the term biotechnology mainly refers to processes that:","image":"","options":["Use naturally occurring microbes only in household fermentation","Use genetically modified organisms to achieve such processes on a larger scale","Use only chemical catalysts without any living material","Use only animals for producing food"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In its modern, restricted sense, biotechnology refers to processes that use genetically modified organisms to carry out such production on a large scale. Household fermentation by natural microbes is biotechnology only in the broad, traditional sense.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-095","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":95,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: By repeating the PCR cycle many times, a DNA segment can be amplified approximately a billion times.\nStatement II: PCR amplification takes place inside living E. coli cells using the host's own DNA polymerase.\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Repeated PCR cycles produce about 1 billion copies, so Statement I is correct. However, PCR is carried out in vitro with an added thermostable polymerase, so Statement II is incorrect. Copying a gene with a host's polymerase inside E. coli describes cloning, not PCR.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-096","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":96,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about agarose gel electrophoresis are correct?\n(a) DNA fragments migrate towards the cathode.\n(b) Agarose is a natural polymer obtained from seaweeds.\n(c) Larger DNA fragments travel farther from the well than smaller ones.\n(d) Ethidium bromide-stained DNA appears as bright orange bands under UV light.\n(e) Cutting out bands and extracting DNA from the gel pieces is called elution.\n(f) Agarose gel electrophoresis can be used to check the progress of a restriction enzyme digestion.","image":"","options":["Three","Four","Five","Six"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statements (b), (d), (e) and (f) are correct. Statement (a) is wrong because DNA moves to the anode. Statement (c) is reversed, since smaller fragments move farther.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-097","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":97,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following gives the correct sequence of steps in a single cycle of PCR?","image":"","options":["Primer annealing → Denaturation → Extension","Extension → Primer annealing → Denaturation","Denaturation → Extension → Primer annealing","Denaturation → Primer annealing → Extension"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The double-stranded DNA must first be separated by heat (denaturation). Primers then bind to complementary regions (annealing), and the polymerase extends them (extension). Primers cannot anneal before the strands are separated.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-098","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":98,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Antibiotics, vaccines and enzymes are examples of products manufactured using bioprocess engineering","Genetic engineering introduces altered genetic material into a host organism, thereby changing its phenotype","Genetic engineering involves techniques that alter the chemistry of DNA only; RNA is never the target of such alteration","Traditional hybridisation cannot by itself prevent undesirable genes from being transferred along with desired ones"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT defines genetic engineering as techniques that alter the chemistry of genetic material, i.e., both DNA and RNA. Excluding RNA makes this statement incorrect. The other three statements agree with the text.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-099","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":99,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"In a continuous culture system, used medium is drained from one side and fresh medium is added from the other. In which phase are the cells kept by this arrangement?","image":"","options":["Lag phase","Log/exponential phase","Stationary phase","Decline phase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Continuous culture keeps cells in the log (exponential) phase, when they are physiologically most active. This gives more biomass and a higher yield of the desired protein.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-100","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":100,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\nA. Restriction endonuclease\nB. DNA ligase\nC. Sticky end\nD. Palindromic sequence\nList-II\n(i) Single-stranded overhang that hydrogen bonds with a complementary cut end\n(ii) Base-pair sequence that reads the same on both strands in the same orientation\n(iii) Cuts both strands in the sugar-phosphate backbone at specific points\n(iv) Joins the ends of cut DNA molecules\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Restriction endonucleases cut the sugar-phosphate backbone and ligase seals cut ends. Sticky ends are overhangs that pair with complementary ends, and a palindrome reads the same on both strands. A common trap is swapping the roles of the cutting enzyme and the joining enzyme.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-101","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":101,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Agarose\n(B) Ethidium bromide\n(C) UV radiation\n(D) Elution\nList-II\n(I) Exposure needed to see stained DNA bands\n(II) Extraction of DNA from excised gel pieces\n(III) Matrix that provides the sieving effect\n(IV) Compound used to stain DNA\nChoose the correct answer:","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Agarose is the sieving matrix and ethidium bromide is the stain. UV exposure makes the stained bands visible, and elution recovers the DNA from the cut gel pieces. A common trap is swapping the stain with the radiation used to view it.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-102","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":102,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Arrange the following steps of recombinant DNA technology in the correct sequence:\nP. Ligation of the DNA fragment into a vector\nQ. Fragmentation of DNA by restriction endonucleases\nR. Isolation of DNA\nS. Transferring the recombinant DNA into the host\nT. Isolation of the desired DNA fragment","image":"","options":["R → T → Q → P → S","Q → R → T → P → S","R → Q → T → P → S","R → Q → P → T → S"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"DNA is first isolated, then cut by restriction enzymes. The desired fragment is then isolated and ligated into a vector, and the recombinant DNA is finally transferred into the host. The desired fragment can only be picked out after cutting, and it must be isolated before ligation.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-103","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":103,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (method of gene transfer) with List-II (description).\nList-I:\nA. Micro-injection\nB. Biolistics\nC. Calcium treatment with heat shock\nD. Disarmed pathogen\nList-II:\nI. Used to make bacterial cells take up plasmid DNA\nII. Recombinant DNA delivered directly into the nucleus of an animal cell\nIII. DNA-coated gold or tungsten particles fired at cells; suited to plants\nIV. Vector infects the cell and transfers recombinant DNA into it","image":"","options":["A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Micro-injection is the nuclear injection into animal cells, and biolistics bombards plant cells with gold or tungsten particles. Ca$^{2+}$ with heat shock transforms bacteria, and disarmed pathogens transfer DNA by infecting the host cell.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-104","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":104,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the polymerase chain reaction are correct?\n(a) Primers are small, chemically synthesised oligonucleotides complementary to regions of the target DNA.\n(b) A single primer is enough to amplify a DNA segment.\n(c) DNA polymerase extends the primers using the nucleotides provided in the reaction, with the genomic DNA as template.\n(d) PCR amplification takes place in vivo inside a host bacterium.\n(e) Repeated cycles can amplify a DNA segment about a billion times.\n(f) The DNA polymerase is destroyed in every cycle, so fresh enzyme must be added before each extension step.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statements (a), (c) and (e) are correct. PCR uses two sets of primers, not one, so (b) is wrong. PCR is an in vitro reaction, so (d) is wrong. The thermostable Taq polymerase survives denaturation and does not need to be replaced each cycle, so (f) is wrong.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-105","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":105,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Hind II was the first restriction endonuclease characterised whose action depended on a specific nucleotide sequence, and it recognises a sequence of six base pairs.\nStatement II: Restriction enzymes cut both DNA strands exactly at the centre of the palindromic site, and this is what produces sticky ends.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct as written. Sticky ends arise because the enzyme cuts a little away from the centre of the palindrome, between the same two bases on opposite strands, which leaves single-stranded overhangs; a cut exactly at the centre would not produce sticky ends.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-106","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":106,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly distinguishes exonucleases from endonucleases?","image":"","options":["Exonucleases cut at specific positions within DNA, while endonucleases remove nucleotides from the ends","Exonucleases remove nucleotides from the ends of DNA, while endonucleases cut at specific positions within DNA","Exonucleases act only on RNA, while endonucleases act only on DNA","Exonucleases join DNA fragments, while endonucleases cut DNA fragments"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Both are nucleases. Exonucleases work from the ends of DNA, while endonucleases cut inside the DNA molecule. Joining DNA fragments is the job of ligase, not of any nuclease.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-107","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":107,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A foreign DNA fragment is ligated at the BamH I site of pBR322, and E. coli is transformed with the ligation mixture. Which statement correctly describes a recombinant colony?","image":"","options":["It grows on both ampicillin and tetracycline media","It grows on tetracycline medium but not on ampicillin medium","It grows on ampicillin medium but not on tetracycline medium","It grows on neither ampicillin nor tetracycline medium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The BamH I site lies inside tetR, so the insert inactivates tetracycline resistance while ampR stays intact. Recombinants therefore grow on ampicillin but not on tetracycline. Non-recombinants grow on both antibiotics.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-108","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":108,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"One strand of the EcoRI recognition site is 5'—GAATTC—3'. Its complementary strand, written in the 3' → 5' direction, is:","image":"","options":["3'—GAATTC—5'","3'—CTTAAG—5'","3'—CAATTG—5'","3'—GTTAAC—5'"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Pairing G–C and A–T base by base gives 3'—CTTAAG—5'. Read 5' → 3', this strand is GAATTC, which is why the site is a palindrome.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-109","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":109,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"In pBR322, the Pst I site lies within the ampicillin resistance gene. A researcher inserts foreign DNA at the Pst I site and transforms E. coli. Which plating strategy correctly identifies the recombinants?","image":"","options":["Plate first on ampicillin, then transfer colonies to tetracycline; recombinants grow on ampicillin but not on tetracycline","Plate first on tetracycline, then transfer colonies to ampicillin; recombinants grow on tetracycline but not on ampicillin","Plate only on ampicillin; recombinants are the colonies that grow","Plate first on tetracycline, then transfer colonies to ampicillin; recombinants grow on both media"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"An insert at Pst I inactivates ampR while tetR stays functional. Tetracycline is therefore used to select transformants, and recombinants are the colonies that fail to grow on ampicillin. The trap is reusing the BamH I scheme, where the two antibiotics play the opposite roles.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-110","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":110,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"In recombinant DNA technology, the abbreviation PCR stands for:","image":"","options":["Protein Chain Reaction","Polymerase Chain Reaction","Primer Cloning Reaction","Plasmid Copying Reaction"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"PCR is the Polymerase Chain Reaction. The enzyme DNA polymerase repeatedly copies a target DNA segment in vitro, which produces a chain of amplification cycles.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-111","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":111,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (enzyme used during DNA isolation) with List-II (what it acts on):\nList-I\nA. Lysozyme\nB. Cellulase\nC. Chitinase\nD. Protease\nList-II\nI. Fungal cell wall\nII. Proteins such as histones associated with DNA\nIII. Bacterial cell wall\nIV. Plant cell wall\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Lysozyme breaks open bacterial cells, cellulase breaks plant cell walls and chitinase breaks fungal walls. Protease removes proteins such as histones that are intertwined with the DNA.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-112","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":112,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"The thermostable DNA polymerase used for repeated amplification in PCR is isolated from:","image":"","options":["Escherichia coli","Agrobacterium tumefaciens","Thermus aquaticus","Salmonella typhimurium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The heat-stable DNA polymerase (Taq polymerase) comes from the bacterium Thermus aquaticus. It stays active at the high temperatures used to denature DNA. The other bacteria appear elsewhere in the chapter but are not the source of this enzyme.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-113","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":113,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the action of EcoRI on its recognition site 5'-GAATTC-3' is incorrect?","image":"","options":["It cuts each of the two strands at a specific point in the sugar-phosphate backbone","It cuts exactly at the centre of the palindrome, producing ends with no single-stranded portions","It cuts between the same two bases on the opposite strands","The cut fragments carry single-stranded overhanging stretches at their ends"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that restriction enzymes such as EcoRI cut a little away from the centre of the palindrome. This leaves single-stranded sticky ends, not ends without overhangs. The other three statements describe the cut correctly.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-114","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":114,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Agarose, the most commonly used matrix in gel electrophoresis of DNA, is:","image":"","options":["A synthetic polymer made from acrylamide","A protein extracted from animal connective tissue","A natural polymer extracted from seaweeds","A polysaccharide extracted from fungal cell walls"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT describes agarose as a natural polymer obtained from seaweeds. Acrylamide is synthetic, and the other sources are incorrect.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-115","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":115,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) $\\beta$-galactosidase coding sequence\n(B) Chromogenic substrate\n(C) T-DNA\n(D) Disarmed retrovirus\nList-II\n(I) Transforms normal plant cells into tumour cells\n(II) Site of insertion leading to insertional inactivation\n(III) Gene delivery vehicle for animal cells\n(IV) Produces blue colour with a functional enzyme","image":"","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The pairings are as follows:\n- Foreign DNA is inserted into the $\\beta$-galactosidase gene, which inactivates it (A-II).\n- The chromogenic substrate turns blue when the enzyme is functional (B-IV).\n- T-DNA causes plant tumours (C-I).\n- Disarmed retroviruses carry genes into animal cells (D-III).","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-116","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":116,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A restriction enzyme has exactly two recognition sites in a circular plasmid and exactly two recognition sites in a linear DNA molecule. Each DNA is completely digested and run in separate lanes of an agarose gel. Assuming all fragments differ in size, how many bands will appear in the plasmid lane and in the linear DNA lane, respectively?","image":"","options":["2 and 2","3 and 3","3 and 2","2 and 3"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Cutting a circle at n sites gives n fragments, while cutting a linear molecule at n sites gives n + 1 fragments. So the plasmid gives 2 bands and the linear DNA gives 3. The trap is assuming both DNAs behave the same way.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-117","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":117,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In agarose gel electrophoresis, smaller DNA fragments are found farther away from the wells than larger fragments.\nReason (R): DNA fragments are negatively charged and therefore migrate towards the anode under an electric field.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Both statements are true. The negative charge explains why DNA moves towards the anode, but not why fragments separate. Separation by size comes from the sieving effect of the agarose gel, which lets smaller fragments travel farther. The trap is to treat the direction of movement as the reason for size-based separation.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-118","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":118,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Small laboratory cultures cannot give appreciable amounts of a recombinant product, so bioreactors were developed. According to NCERT, these bioreactors can typically process culture volumes of about:","image":"","options":["1–10 litres","10–50 millilitres","100–1000 litres","10,000–1,00,000 litres"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Bioreactors were developed to handle large culture volumes of 100–1000 litres, which give large-scale yields of the product. Volumes in millilitres or a few litres are small laboratory or shake-flask scales.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-119","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":119,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"After the biosynthetic stage, the processes of separation and purification of the product are collectively referred to as:","image":"","options":["Upstream processing","Elution","Downstream processing","Insertional inactivation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Separation and purification of the product after biosynthesis together make up downstream processing. Elution is extracting DNA from an agarose gel piece, which is a different step.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-120","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":120,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A researcher wants a foreign gene to be multiplied and passed on to the progeny of a bacterial host. Which approach is most consistent with the principle used by Cohen and Boyer?","image":"","options":["Join the gene to a plasmid that has an origin of replication, using cuts made by a restriction enzyme and joining by DNA ligase, then transfer it into the host","Introduce the naked gene fragment alone so that the host's DNA polymerase copies it directly","Remove the origin of replication from the plasmid before inserting the gene so the gene is not diluted","Treat the gene with DNA ligase alone so it circularises and replicates without any vector"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"An alien gene multiplies only when it is linked to an origin of replication, which an autonomously replicating plasmid vector provides. Restriction enzymes and ligase are used to build this construct. A naked fragment, or a construct without an ori, would not be maintained in the progeny.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-121","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":121,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the first recombinant DNA experiment (1972) are correct?\n(a) The native plasmid used belonged to Salmonella typhimurium.\n(b) The antibiotic resistance gene was cut out of a plasmid using restriction enzymes.\n(c) The recombinant DNA molecule was formed inside the E. coli cell.\n(d) In E. coli, the recombinant DNA replicated using the host's DNA polymerase.\n(e) The gene was joined to the plasmid by DNA polymerase.","image":"","options":["Two","Four","Five","Three"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statements (a), (b) and (d) are correct. The recombinant DNA was created in vitro, not inside E. coli, so (c) is wrong. The joining was done by DNA ligase, not DNA polymerase, so (e) is wrong.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-122","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":122,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The EFB definition of biotechnology covers only modern molecular biotechnology and excludes traditional microbe-based processes.\nStatement II: Genetic engineering and bioprocess engineering are the two core techniques that enabled the birth of modern biotechnology.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The EFB definition deliberately covers both the traditional view and modern molecular biotechnology, so Statement I is wrong. Statement II correctly names the two core techniques behind modern biotechnology.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-123","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":123,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A DNA polymerase isolated from Thermus aquaticus is preferred for PCR.\nReason (R): This enzyme remains active during the high-temperature denaturation of double-stranded DNA, so the amplification cycles can be repeated.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"PCR repeatedly heats the DNA to separate its strands. An ordinary polymerase would be destroyed by this heat. The thermostability of the Thermus aquaticus enzyme is exactly why it allows repeated cycles, so R correctly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-124","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":124,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following double-stranded DNA sequences is a palindrome, that is, reads the same on both strands when each is read in the 5' → 3' direction? (Only the upper strand is shown.)","image":"","options":["5'-GGATTC-3'","5'-GGATCC-3'","5'-GAAGTC-3'","5'-AAGCCT-3'"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The complementary strand of 5'-GGATCC-3', read 5' → 3', is also GGATCC, so this sequence is a DNA palindrome. The other sequences give different complementary sequences, for example GGATTC pairs with 5'-GAATCC-3'.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-125","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":125,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"In the E. coli cloning vector pBR322, what does the gene labelled 'rop' code for?","image":"","options":["Proteins involved in the replication of the plasmid","An enzyme that inactivates ampicillin","A restriction endonuclease that cuts at the BamH I site","A protein that pumps tetracycline out of the cell"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In pBR322, rop codes for the proteins involved in plasmid replication. Resistance to ampicillin and tetracycline is given by ampR and tetR, not by rop.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-126","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":126,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are present in the E. coli cloning vector pBR322, as shown in the NCERT map?\nori, rop, ampR, tetR, T-DNA, kanamycin resistance gene","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"pBR322 carries ori, rop, ampR and tetR, which makes four. T-DNA belongs to the Ti plasmid of Agrobacterium. Kanamycin resistance is a useful E. coli marker in general, but it is not part of pBR322.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-127","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":127,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Thermus aquaticus\n(B) Agarose\n(C) Ethidium bromide\n(D) Chilled ethanol\nList-II\n(I) Visualisation of DNA bands under UV light\n(II) Precipitation of purified DNA\n(III) Source of thermostable DNA polymerase\n(IV) Natural polymer obtained from seaweeds\nChoose the correct answer:","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Thermus aquaticus supplies the heat-stable polymerase used in PCR, and agarose is extracted from seaweeds. Ethidium bromide stains DNA so it appears as orange bands under UV light. Chilled ethanol precipitates purified DNA as fine threads.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-128","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":128,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Traditional hybridisation in plant and animal breeding often leads to the inclusion and multiplication of undesirable genes along with the desired genes.\nReason (R): Genetic engineering allows only one or a set of desirable genes to be isolated and introduced into a target organism.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Both statements are true. However, R describes how genetic engineering overcomes the limitation stated in A; it does not explain why hybridisation carries undesirable genes. So R is not the explanation of A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-129","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":129,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Match each part of the enzyme name EcoRI in List-I with what it represents in List-II.\nList-I\n(A) E\n(B) co\n(C) R\n(D) I\nList-II\n(I) Strain of the bacterium\n(II) Order of isolation from that strain\n(III) Genus of the bacterium\n(IV) Species of the bacterium","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The first letter comes from the genus (Escherichia) and the next two letters from the species (coli). 'R' comes from the strain RY 13, and the Roman numeral 'I' shows the order of isolation from that strain.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-130","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":130,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Which two antibiotic resistance genes are present in the cloning vector pBR322?","image":"","options":["Kanamycin and chloramphenicol resistance genes","Ampicillin and kanamycin resistance genes","Tetracycline and chloramphenicol resistance genes","Ampicillin and tetracycline resistance genes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"pBR322 carries ampR and tetR. Kanamycin and chloramphenicol resistance are also valid markers for E. coli, but they are not part of pBR322.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-131","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":131,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When a chromogenic substrate is used, bacterial colonies carrying a recombinant plasmid remain colourless.\nReason (R): The alien DNA is inserted into the rop gene, which codes for the enzyme that produces blue colour.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Recombinant colonies are colourless because the insert inactivates the β-galactosidase gene. The rop gene codes for proteins involved in plasmid replication, not for a colour-producing enzyme, so R is false.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-132","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":132,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A cloning vector carries an ampicillin resistance gene and the coding sequence for β-galactosidase, with the cloning site lying inside the β-galactosidase gene. After a ligation reaction, E. coli cells are transformed and plated on a medium containing ampicillin and a chromogenic substrate. Which of the following inferences is correct?","image":"","options":["Blue colonies contain plasmids carrying the foreign DNA insert","Colourless colonies arise from transformed cells whose β-galactosidase gene has been inactivated by the insert","Untransformed E. coli cells form colourless colonies because they lack the plasmid-borne β-galactosidase gene","To distinguish recombinants, this method still requires plating on two plates containing different antibiotics"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Only transformed cells survive ampicillin. Among them, an insert inside the β-galactosidase gene stops colour formation, so colourless colonies are the recombinants and blue colonies carry vectors without an insert. Untransformed cells do not grow at all, and this colour-based method was developed precisely to avoid the cumbersome two-plate antibiotic procedure.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-133","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":133,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"In the context of DNA, a sequence is called palindromic when it:","image":"","options":["reads the same on the two strands when both are read in the same orientation (e.g., 5' → 3')","reads the same forward and backward on a single strand, like the word MALAYALAM","contains only A and T bases arranged symmetrically","is repeated many times in tandem along the chromosome"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A DNA palindrome reads the same on both strands when the reading direction is kept the same, as in 5'-GAATTC-3' and its partner strand 5'-GAATTC-3'. The trap is the word-palindrome idea of reading one strand backward.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-134","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":134,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about introducing recombinant DNA into host cells are correct?\n(a) In biolistics, plant cells are bombarded with high-velocity gold or tungsten particles coated with DNA.\n(b) In micro-injection, recombinant DNA is injected directly into the cytoplasm of a plant cell.\n(c) Bacteria take up DNA after incubation with DNA on ice, a brief heat shock at $42^\\circ$C, and return to ice.\n(d) Bacterial cells are made competent by treating them with a monovalent cation such as sodium.\n(e) Disarmed retroviruses are used to deliver desirable genes into animal cells.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statements (a), (c) and (e) are correct. Micro-injection delivers DNA into the nucleus of an animal cell, not into a plant cell's cytoplasm. Competence is induced by a divalent cation such as calcium, not sodium.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-135","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":135,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The overhanging single-stranded ends produced by EcoRI are called sticky ends because they can form hydrogen bonds with their complementary cut counterparts.\nStatement II: The stickiness of these ends hinders the action of DNA ligase.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Sticky ends are named for their ability to hydrogen bond with complementary ends. This pairing holds the fragments together and helps DNA ligase work, so Statement II is false.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-136","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":136,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"To monitor a restriction digestion, a student runs an agarose gel. Lane 1 has the undigested DNA, and lanes 2–4 have samples taken from the digestion reaction. After ethidium bromide staining and UV exposure, lane 1 shows a single band close to the wells, while lanes 2–4 show several bands spread further down the gel. Which conclusion is correct?","image":"","options":["The DNA in lanes 2–4 has not been cut, because cut DNA cannot be stained","The bands farthest from the wells are the largest fragments","The enzyme has cut the DNA into fragments, and the smaller fragments have moved farther from the wells","The single band in lane 1 is RNA left over because ribonuclease treatment was skipped"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Undigested DNA stays as one large band near the loading wells. Digestion produces several fragments that the agarose separates by size through its sieving effect, so smaller pieces travel farther. The trap is assuming that distance travelled increases with fragment size.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-137","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":137,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When plated with a chromogenic substrate, bacterial colonies carrying a plasmid without an insert in the $\\beta$-galactosidase gene appear blue.\nReason (R): In such plasmids the $\\beta$-galactosidase coding sequence is intact, so the enzyme is produced and acts on the chromogenic substrate to give colour.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Non-recombinant plasmids have a functional $\\beta$-galactosidase gene, so the enzyme converts the chromogenic substrate into a blue product. In recombinants the insert inactivates the gene, so their colonies stay colourless.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-138","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":138,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (component of pBR322) with List-II (its role/description).\nList-I:\nA. ori\nB. ampR\nC. rop\nD. BamH I\nList-II:\nI. Codes for proteins involved in plasmid replication\nII. Restriction site within the tetracycline resistance gene\nIII. Sequence where replication starts; controls copy number\nIV. Selectable marker conferring ampicillin resistance","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"ori starts replication and controls copy number. ampR is the ampicillin marker, rop codes for replication proteins, and BamH I is a cloning site inside tetR. A common trap is to swap ori and rop, since both relate to replication.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-139","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":139,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"In the first construction of an artificial recombinant DNA molecule by Stanley Cohen and Herbert Boyer in 1972, the antibiotic resistance gene was linked with a native plasmid of which organism?","image":"","options":["Escherichia coli","Salmonella typhimurium","Agrobacterium tumifaciens","Thermus aquaticus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Cohen and Boyer linked the antibiotic resistance gene to a native plasmid of Salmonella typhimurium. E. coli was only the host into which the recombinant plasmid was later transferred, which makes it the tempting wrong choice.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-140","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":140,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\nA. Stirrer of a stirred-tank bioreactor\nB. Sparger\nC. Sampling port\nD. Downstream processing\nList-II\nI. Periodic withdrawal of small volumes of culture\nII. Separation and purification of the product\nIII. Even mixing and oxygen availability throughout the vessel\nIV. Bubbling sterile air through the culture\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The stirrer gives even mixing and oxygen availability, the sparger bubbles sterile air, sampling ports let small volumes be drawn periodically, and downstream processing means separation and purification. A common confusion is to swap the roles of the stirrer and the sparger.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-141","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":141,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Before transformation, bacterial cells are treated with a specific concentration of calcium ions.\nReason (R): DNA is a hydrophilic molecule and cannot pass through cell membranes on its own.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"DNA is hydrophilic and cannot cross membranes by itself. The cells are therefore made 'competent' with a divalent cation such as calcium, which increases DNA entry through pores in the cell wall. R directly explains why A is done.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-142","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":142,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"When a restriction endonuclease cuts DNA at its recognition site, the bonds it breaks are located in the:","image":"","options":["hydrogen bonds between complementary bases","sugar-phosphate backbones of both strands","glycosidic bonds linking bases to sugars","peptide bonds of histone proteins"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"After binding its recognition sequence, the enzyme cuts each of the two strands at specific points in the sugar-phosphate backbone. These are phosphodiester bonds, not hydrogen bonds between bases.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-143","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":143,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A continuous culture system gives a higher yield of the desired recombinant protein than a simple small-scale culture.\nReason (R): In a continuous culture, the used medium is drained while fresh medium is added, so cells remain in their log phase and produce more biomass.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Continuous medium replacement keeps cells in the most active exponential phase. This produces larger biomass and therefore a higher protein yield, so R directly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-144","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":144,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In agarose gel electrophoresis, larger DNA fragments travel farther from the well than smaller fragments.\nStatement II: DNA bands in a gel can be seen as bright orange bands only after staining with ethidium bromide and exposing the gel to UV light.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The agarose gel acts as a sieve, so smaller fragments move farther. Statement I is therefore wrong. Pure DNA cannot be seen in visible light, so it must be stained with ethidium bromide and viewed under UV, where the bands look orange.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-145","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":145,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (step in DNA isolation and cutting) with List-II (agent or technique used).\nList-I\nA. Removal of histones associated with DNA\nB. Removal of RNA from the cell extract\nC. Precipitation of purified DNA as fine threads\nD. Checking the progress of a restriction digestion\nList-II\nI. Chilled ethanol\nII. Agarose gel electrophoresis\nIII. Protease\nIV. Ribonuclease","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Histones are proteins and are removed by protease, while RNA is removed by ribonuclease. Purified DNA comes out of solution as threads when chilled ethanol is added, and these threads can be spooled. Agarose gel electrophoresis is used to monitor how far a restriction digestion has progressed. The key trap is swapping protease and ribonuclease.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-146","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":146,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A PCR is started with a single double-stranded DNA molecule containing the target gene. Assume every cycle doubles the number of target DNA molecules. Approximately how many copies will be present after 30 cycles?","image":"","options":["60","900","About $10^{9}$","About $10^{30}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Doubling each cycle gives $2^{30} \\approx 1.07 \\times 10^{9}$ copies, which matches the 'about a billion copies' figure in NCERT. The value 60 assumes linear addition, 900 comes from squaring 30, and $10^{30}$ wrongly treats 30 as a power of ten.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-147","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":147,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"In a continuous culture system, used medium is drained out from one side while fresh medium is added from the other. This keeps the cells in which growth phase?","image":"","options":["Lag phase","Log (exponential) phase","Stationary phase","Decline (death) phase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Replacing used medium with fresh medium maintains cells in the log or exponential phase, when they are physiologically most active. The trap is the stationary phase, which a closed culture eventually reaches.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-148","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":148,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When DNA is isolated from animal tissue, the lysate is treated with protease.\nReason (R): Genes lie on long DNA molecules that are intertwined with proteins such as histones.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"DNA must be pure before restriction enzymes can cut it. Because it is associated with histones and other proteins, protease is used to digest these proteins away, so R directly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-149","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":149,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\nA. Year in which two phage-restricting enzymes of E. coli were isolated\nB. Length of the recognition sequence of Hind II (in base pairs)\nC. Number of restriction enzymes known today (more than)\nD. Number of bacterial strains from which restriction enzymes have been isolated (over)\nList-II\n(i) 6\n(ii) 230\n(iii) 900\n(iv) 1963\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iii)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The phage-restricting enzymes were isolated in 1963, and Hind II recognises a 6 bp sequence. More than 900 restriction enzymes are known, isolated from over 230 bacterial strains. The common trap is swapping the 900 and 230 figures.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-150","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":150,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, bioreactors were developed so that large volumes of culture could be processed. What is the typical volume range of such bioreactors?","image":"","options":["1–10 litres","10–50 litres","100–1000 litres","10,000–100,000 litres"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Bioreactors process large culture volumes of about 100–1000 litres. Small laboratory cultures cannot give appreciable amounts of product.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-151","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":151,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A researcher finds a nuclease that steadily degrades linear DNA fragments but leaves an intact, covalently closed circular plasmid completely undigested. What is the most appropriate classification of this nuclease?","image":"","options":["An endonuclease, because it cuts at specific positions within the DNA","A restriction endonuclease, because it recognises a palindromic sequence","An exonuclease, because it needs free DNA ends to remove nucleotides","A DNA ligase, because it acts only on cut DNA molecules"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Exonucleases remove nucleotides from the ends of DNA. A closed circular plasmid has no free ends, so it is not degraded. An endonuclease cuts within the DNA and could have cut the plasmid if its site were present, and a ligase joins DNA rather than degrading it.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-152","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":152,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"In which method of gene transfer is recombinant DNA directly injected into the nucleus of an animal cell?","image":"","options":["Biolistics","Heat shock transformation","Disarmed pathogen vector","Micro-injection"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"In micro-injection, recombinant DNA is injected straight into the nucleus of an animal cell. Biolistics fires DNA-coated particles and is suited to plants, while heat shock is used for bacteria.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-153","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":153,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following gives the correct sequence of steps for forcing recombinant DNA into competent bacterial cells?","image":"","options":["Incubate cells with recombinant DNA at 42°C → place on ice → return to 42°C","Treat cells with Ca$^{2+}$ → incubate with recombinant DNA on ice → brief exposure to 42°C → back on ice","Incubate cells with recombinant DNA on ice → treat with Ca$^{2+}$ at 42°C for a long period → keep at room temperature","Brief exposure to 42°C → treat with Ca$^{2+}$ → incubate with recombinant DNA on ice"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"First the cells are made competent with a divalent cation. They are then incubated with recombinant DNA on ice, given a brief heat shock at 42°C, and returned to ice. The heat shock is brief, not prolonged.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-154","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":154,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A foreign DNA fragment is ligated at the BamH I site of pBR322, and the recombinant plasmid is introduced into E. coli. What growth pattern will the bacteria carrying this recombinant plasmid show?","image":"","options":["Growth on both ampicillin and tetracycline media","Growth on ampicillin medium but not on tetracycline medium","Growth on tetracycline medium but not on ampicillin medium","No growth on either ampicillin or tetracycline medium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The BamH I site lies within tetR, so the insert inactivates tetracycline resistance while ampR stays intact. Growth on both media is the pattern of non-recombinant transformants.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-155","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":155,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A researcher wants to isolate DNA from a fungus. Which enzyme should be used first to break open the cells?","image":"","options":["Lysozyme","Cellulase","Chitinase","Ribonuclease"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Fungal cell walls contain chitin, so chitinase is used. Lysozyme is used for bacteria and cellulase for plant cells. Ribonuclease only removes RNA after the cells have been opened.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-156","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":156,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"To isolate DNA from a fungal mycelium, the cell wall must first be broken. Which enzyme is most suitable for this step?","image":"","options":["Lysozyme","Cellulase","Chitinase","Ribonuclease"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Fungal cell walls are made largely of chitin, so chitinase is used to break them. Lysozyme is used for bacterial cells and cellulase for plant cells. Ribonuclease only removes RNA after the cell is opened.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-157","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":157,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Genes for resistance to ampicillin, chloramphenicol, tetracycline or kanamycin are useful selectable markers for E. coli mainly because:","image":"","options":["these antibiotics are produced by E. coli itself","these genes increase the copy number of the plasmid","normal E. coli cells do not carry resistance against these antibiotics","these genes contain multiple restriction sites for cloning"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Normal E. coli cells lack resistance to these antibiotics. So only cells that received the vector carrying the resistance gene survive on the antibiotic medium, and non-transformants are eliminated.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-158","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":158,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) Herbert Boyer\n(B) Stanley Cohen\n(C) Escherichia coli\n(D) Origin of replication\nList-II:\n(I) Host in which the first recombinant plasmid was multiplied\n(II) Restriction enzymes producing sticky ends\n(III) Sequence responsible for initiating replication\n(IV) Method of removing plasmids from cells and reinserting them into other cells","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-I, B-IV, C-II, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Boyer studied E. coli restriction enzymes that leave sticky ends, and Cohen developed plasmid removal and reinsertion. E. coli was the host for the recombinant DNA, and the origin of replication initiates replication.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-159","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":159,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"The progress of a restriction enzyme digestion is checked by agarose gel electrophoresis. Towards which electrode do the DNA fragments move, and why?","image":"","options":["Towards the cathode, because DNA is positively charged","Towards the anode, because DNA is negatively charged","Towards the anode, because DNA is positively charged","Towards the cathode, because DNA is negatively charged"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The phosphate groups give DNA a negative charge, so it moves towards the positive electrode (anode). Options pairing the anode or cathode with the wrong charge are inconsistent with basic electrostatics.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-160","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":160,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A stirred-tank bioreactor is usually cylindrical or has a curved base so that its contents mix properly.\nStatement II: In a sparged stirred-tank bioreactor, sterile air bubbles are passed through the culture.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The cylindrical shape or curved base helps mix the reactor contents. In the sparged design, sterile air is bubbled through the culture as an alternative or addition to stirring for oxygen supply.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-161","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":161,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"In the name of the restriction enzyme EcoRI, what does the letter 'R' stand for?","image":"","options":["The genus of the bacterium","The strain of the bacterium","The order in which the enzyme was isolated","The recognition sequence of the enzyme"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"EcoRI comes from Escherichia coli RY 13. 'Eco' comes from the genus and species, and 'R' comes from the strain name RY 13. The order of isolation is shown by the Roman numeral, not by 'R'.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-162","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":162,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A company grows recombinant E. coli in a closed vessel with a fixed amount of medium. Product output per run levels off because the cells stop growing actively as nutrients are used up and wastes build up. Which change, based on NCERT, would most directly increase the yield of the recombinant protein?","image":"","options":["Adding a second selectable marker gene to the plasmid","Switching to continuous culture, draining spent medium while adding fresh medium","Increasing the frequency of withdrawal through sampling ports","Beginning downstream processing before the culture reaches its peak density"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Continuous culture replaces spent medium with fresh medium and holds cells in the log phase. This gives larger biomass and higher protein yield. Selectable markers, sampling and earlier purification do not tackle the falling growth rate.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-163","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":163,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When foreign DNA is ligated at the BamH I site of the cloning vector pBR322, the transformed recombinant E. coli cells grow on an ampicillin medium but fail to grow on a tetracycline medium.\nReason (R): The BamH I site lies within the tetracycline resistance gene, and inserting foreign DNA there inactivates that gene.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The insert disrupts tetR, so recombinants lose tetracycline resistance. Their ampR gene is intact, so they still grow on ampicillin. Insertional inactivation of tetR is exactly why the recombinants behave this way.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-164","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":164,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): To create a recombinant vector, the vector DNA and the source DNA are normally cut with the same restriction enzyme.\nReason (R): DNA fragments cut by the same restriction enzyme carry the same kind of sticky ends, which can be joined end-to-end by DNA ligase.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The same enzyme generates matching sticky ends on both vector and insert. These ends pair and are then sealed by ligase, so R directly explains why A is necessary.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-165","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":165,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"To make E. coli cells 'competent' for taking up recombinant DNA, they are treated with a specific concentration of:","image":"","options":["A monovalent cation such as sodium","A divalent anion such as sulphate","A divalent cation such as calcium","The enzyme lysozyme to remove the cell wall"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A divalent cation such as calcium increases how efficiently DNA enters the bacterium through pores in its cell wall. Lysozyme is used to break cells open during DNA isolation, not to make them competent.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-166","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":166,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In agarose gel electrophoresis, DNA fragments are separated mainly on the basis of their size.\nStatement II: Pure DNA fragments in the gel can be seen directly under visible light without any staining.\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Separation is by size because of the sieving effect of agarose. However, pure DNA is invisible in visible light. It must be stained with ethidium bromide and viewed under UV light.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-167","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":167,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"The first artificial recombinant DNA molecule was constructed by linking an antibiotic resistance gene with a native plasmid of which organism?","image":"","options":["Escherichia coli","Agrobacterium tumefaciens","Salmonella typhimurium","Thermus aquaticus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Cohen and Boyer (1972) linked the antibiotic resistance gene to a native plasmid of Salmonella typhimurium. E. coli was the host into which the recombinant DNA was later transferred, which is the common trap.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-168","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":168,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Suppose three different restriction enzymes are isolated, one after another, from strain H of the bacterium Bacillus amyloliquefaciens. Following the standard naming convention, what would the third enzyme be called?","image":"","options":["BaHIII","BamHIII","BamIIIH","AmyBHIII"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The name is built as follows: 'B' from the genus, 'am' from the first two letters of the species, 'H' from the strain, and finally a Roman numeral for the order of isolation. That gives BamHIII. The other options either use too few species letters or put the parts in the wrong order.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-169","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":169,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"The first restriction endonuclease whose action depended on a specific nucleotide sequence was Hind II. Its recognition sequence is made up of how many base pairs?","image":"","options":["Four","Five","Six","Eight"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Hind II always cuts DNA at a particular point after recognising a specific sequence of six base pairs. This sequence is its recognition sequence.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-170","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":170,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Which organism naturally delivers a piece of DNA called 'T-DNA' into the cells of several dicot plants and turns them into tumour cells?","image":"","options":["Escherichia coli","Thermus aquaticus","Agrobacterium tumefaciens","Salmonella typhimurium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Agrobacterium tumefaciens is a pathogen of many dicots. It transfers T-DNA from its Ti plasmid into plant cells and causes tumours. Thermus aquaticus is the source of the thermostable polymerase used in PCR, not a plant pathogen.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-171","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":171,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about large-scale production of a recombinant protein:\nA. In a continuous culture, used medium is drained from one side and fresh medium is added from the other, keeping cells in the log/exponential phase.\nB. A stirred-tank bioreactor is usually cylindrical or has a curved base to help mix its contents.\nC. In a sparged stirred-tank bioreactor, sterile air bubbles are passed through the culture.\nD. Downstream processing is the biosynthetic stage in which host cells synthesise the recombinant protein inside the bioreactor.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A, B and C only","A, C and D only","B and D only","A, B, C and D"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statements A, B and C correctly describe continuous culture and stirred-tank design. Downstream processing happens after the biosynthetic stage and includes separation, purification, formulation and quality control, so D is wrong.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-172","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":172,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A ligation mixture containing pBR322 cut at the BamH I site and joined with foreign DNA was used to transform E. coli. The cells were spread on an ampicillin plate, which gave 240 colonies. These colonies were replica-plated onto a tetracycline plate, where 180 colonies grew at matching positions. How many of the original colonies carry recombinant plasmids?","image":"","options":["60","180","240","420"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"All 240 colonies on ampicillin are transformants. The 180 that also grow on tetracycline still have an intact tetR, so they are non-recombinants. The remaining 240 − 180 = 60 colonies lost tetracycline resistance through insertional inactivation and are the recombinants.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-173","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":173,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Normally, a recombinant vector molecule cannot be created unless the vector and the source DNA are cut with the same restriction enzyme.\nStatement II: A restriction enzyme cuts both strands a little away from the centre of the palindrome but between the same two bases, so all fragments it produces carry complementary sticky ends that can pair and be sealed by DNA ligase.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Both statements are correct. Cutting with the same enzyme gives vector and insert matching sticky ends, which form hydrogen bonds with each other and help DNA ligase join them. Statement II explains why Statement I holds.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-174","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":174,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"According to present knowledge, roughly how many restriction enzymes have been isolated, and from how many bacterial strains?","image":"","options":["More than 230 enzymes from over 900 strains","More than 90 enzymes from over 23 strains","More than 900 enzymes from over 230 strains","More than 9000 enzymes from over 2300 strains"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"More than 900 restriction enzymes have been isolated from over 230 bacterial strains, and each recognises a different sequence. The first option is a trap because it swaps the two numbers.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-175","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":175,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Why is selecting recombinants by inactivation of an antibiotic resistance gene considered a cumbersome procedure?","image":"","options":["It requires plating on two plates containing different antibiotics","It requires heat shock at $42^\\circ$C for every colony","It needs ethidium bromide staining of each colony","It works only with plant cells and not with bacteria"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Recombinants are recognised only by growing the transformants on two separate media, for example ampicillin and then tetracycline. This double plating is laborious, which led to the development of chromogenic (colour-based) selection.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-176","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":176,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["In both the antibiotic-based and the chromogenic methods, recombinants are recognised because the inserted DNA disrupts a gene present in the vector","Chromogenic selection distinguishes recombinants from non-recombinants without plating on two different antibiotic media","Retroviruses used as vectors for animal cells retain their ability to convert normal cells into cancerous cells","The idea of using Ti plasmids and retroviruses as vectors came from understanding how pathogens deliver genes to their eukaryotic hosts"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Retroviral vectors are disarmed: their disease-causing ability is removed and only their gene-delivery capacity is kept. Statement 3 is therefore incorrect. The trap is that natural retroviruses can transform cells, but the vectors cannot.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-177","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":177,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Suppose a foreign gene is inserted into a restriction site lying within the ampR gene of pBR322, and the tetR gene is left intact. Which strategy would correctly separate recombinants from non-recombinants?","image":"","options":["Plate transformants on ampicillin; recombinants are the colonies that grow","Plate transformants on tetracycline, then replica-plate onto ampicillin; recombinants are colonies that fail to grow on ampicillin","Plate transformants on ampicillin, then replica-plate onto tetracycline; recombinants are colonies that fail to grow on tetracycline","Plate transformants on a medium with both antibiotics; recombinants are the colonies that grow"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Here the insert inactivates ampR, so tetR becomes the marker for selecting transformants. On replica plating, recombinants grow on tetracycline but not on ampicillin, while non-recombinants grow on both. This reverses the BamH I (tetR) case described in NCERT.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-178","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":178,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A foreign gene is ligated at the BamH I site of the vector pBR322, and the plasmids are used to transform E. coli. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(a) Recombinant transformants grow on a medium containing ampicillin.\n(b) Recombinant transformants grow on a medium containing tetracycline.\n(c) Non-recombinant transformants grow on media containing either antibiotic.\n(d) Untransformed E. coli cells grow on a medium containing ampicillin.\n(e) The rop gene of pBR322 codes for proteins involved in plasmid replication.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statements (a), (c) and (e) are correct. The BamH I site lies in the tetracycline-resistance gene, so recombinants lose tetracycline resistance but keep ampicillin resistance. Normal E. coli cells carry no resistance to these antibiotics, so untransformed cells cannot grow on ampicillin.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-179","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":179,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(a) Selection using a chromogenic substrate avoids the cumbersome need to plate on two plates containing different antibiotics.\n(b) Blue colonies on a chromogenic substrate medium carry plasmids with the foreign DNA insert.\n(c) Agrobacterium tumefaciens is a pathogen of several monocot plants and transfers T-DNA into them.\n(d) The modified Ti plasmid is no longer pathogenic to plants but still uses the mechanism of Agrobacterium to deliver genes.\n(e) Retroviruses can naturally transform normal animal cells into cancerous cells, and disarmed retroviruses are used as vectors for animal cells.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statements (a), (d) and (e) are correct. Statement (b) is wrong because blue colonies have an intact β-galactosidase gene, which means they carry no insert. Statement (c) is wrong because A. tumefaciens is a pathogen of dicot plants, not monocots.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-180","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":180,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Selection of recombinants using a chromogenic substrate is preferred over selection based on inactivation of antibiotic resistance genes.\nReason (R): Selection based on inactivation of antibiotic resistance requires plating on only a single plate containing one antibiotic.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true because antibiotic-based selection of recombinants is cumbersome. It requires plating on two plates with different antibiotics, so R is false. Chromogenic selection separates colonies by colour: blue colonies have no insert, and colourless colonies are recombinants.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"}]});
